rom  from Ryan that it would never be told  from Dahmer's point of view you this  lines up with a subtle focus on all the  other characters portrayed in the show  you might notice that the stories with  the most detail are those of the people  Dahmer affected rather than Dahmer  himself Peters also says that as an  audience you're not really sympathizing  with him you're not really getting into  his plight you're more sort of watching  it number four you might notice first on  the Netflix  chilling visual Evan Peters wore yellow  contacts for the role while we might be  accustomed to alarming choices like this  on screen we're surprised to learn  through the characters on-screen  explanation that Dahmer had a real life  fascination with yellow eyes in the  second episode when Dahmer anticipates  conorack in his apartment the victim  wakes up in Dahmer's lap to everyone's  surprise Dahmer is wearing yellow  contacts he explains that they're like  the Emperors in Star Wars Episode 4  Return of the Jedi  number five it seems the making of this  show wasn't just for entertainment it  might also have been for telling the  stories of all those people affected by  Dahmer and his acts this seems to have  been director Ryan Murphy's approach and  we can hear hints of it through the  actors involved in the production actor  Richard Jenkins who plays Dahmer's  father said this about director Ryan  Murphy's approach to the work Ryan used  to be a journalist and it's really  thorough an investigation not just of  Dahmer but the damage that he caused to  the victims and their families and to  his parents number six it wasn't super  cheery  in one of a few videos from on set we  see a near expressionless Evan Peters in  his Dahmer costume and hair leaving them  all with a camera rolling in front of  him the moment feels plain and Bleak was  this the feeling on set all the time we  hope not there is some hope though after  Penelope Miller played Dahmer's mother  on the show and was one of the few to  post some photos from the set on her  socials Penelope described working with  actor Evan Peters in her post I beyond  loved working with him and even though  Jeffrey and Joyce Dahmer had a strained  and estranged relationship in the true  story We secretly wished we had more to  do together he is a consummate actor and  I adore him so it can't have been all  bad you haven't had a nice word for me  in years you're always in your room  you don't want a mother you don't want  me number seven this one's for you  Glenda actor Niecy Nash talks about how  they decided to dedicate a part of the  show to the character of Glenda  Cleveland one of the surviving victims  Glenda did a lot in real life but not  many people who are aware of the story  know that much about her Niecy  discovered while working on Dahmer that  Glenda regularly reported Dahmer to the  police in real life and no one really  heeded her warnings misi expressed a  very pivotal moment  and see the family of this baby she  tried to help she wonders if it all  could have been prevented if the police  had listened to Glenda at least in this  show her work actually got recognized  number eight how do you get into the  state of mind to play Someone Like  Jeffrey Dahmer on film if you ask the  actor Evan Peters he might say research  and then proceeded to  read  biographies Peter seems to have delved  deep into the behavior of this character  saying Dahmer is interesting and that he  is almost regretful and has guilt and  sort of confusion about what went on he  doesn't really have a Charming  mischievous smile he is docile sort of  aloof and almost disassociated from what  he did Peters adds that during his  research he found the way that Dahmer  spoke was very candid and very normal  again it was so jaw-dropping that it all  really happened number nine before  signing on to the project Peters was  sent the scripts by director Ryan Murphy  Murphy also sent him recommendations to  learn more 